          explanation: '"Sakhāyaṃ mā tyaktam" — do not abandon your friends. The Kanva poets address Indra with the intimacy of sakhi — friendship. This is theologically radical: not "do not abandon your worshippers" or "your servants" but "your friends." Friendship implies mutuality, reciprocity, genuine care. "Sa hi naḥ sakhā prathamaḥ" — you are our first and foremost sakha: Indra as the primary friendship, the foundational relationship from which all other relationships draw their meaning. In the Bhagavad Gita, Krishna calls himself Arjuna\'s sakha — the tradition traces directly back here. The divine as the friend who does not abandon — who is present in difficulty, not just in celebration.',
          science: 'Research on friendship, social connection, and health (Holt-Lunstad on loneliness as a mortality risk; Cacioppo on the neuroscience of social connection): social isolation is now ranked alongside smoking as a mortality risk factor. The subjective experience of being befriended — of having a "sakhā prathamaḥ," a primary companion who will not abandon — is one of the strongest predictors of psychological resilience, physical health, and lifespan. The Kanva poets\' insistence on the divine as sakha is the ancient articulation of what modern research calls the felt sense of secure attachment to something larger than oneself.',
          lifeLesson: '"Sakhāyaṃ mā tyaktam" — do not abandon us. The Kanva hymn reveals something about the nature of genuine prayer: it is not always praise, not always petition, sometimes it is simply the cry not to be left alone. In your most difficult moments — when the ground feels uncertain and the familiar structures have failed — this prayer is available: "Do not abandon me. You are my first companion." Not as magical thinking but as the reorientation of attention toward the foundational presence that the tradition claims is always already there.',
